    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our offensive core is anchored by **Josh Naylor** (77.11), whose matchup against Scherzer presents the highest ceiling on the board. The 5.40 xERA of the opposing arm, coupled with a significant boost for his #5 spot in the order, positions **Josh Naylor** to drive significant run production. We also see high-leverage value in **Mickey Moniak** (66.59) and **Brice Turang** (66.14). The latter\u2019s aggressive leadoff role combined with a +10% order bonus makes **Brice Turang** an essential engine for our scoring, while **Henry Bolte** (65.39) benefits similarly from the assumed leadoff spot against Bassitt, leveraging the +10% order factor and park environment to secure a starting nod despite the pending lineup verification.\n\nStrategic platoon advantages and speed profiles are driving the remainder of the lineup. **Jose Caballero** (62.37) is a must-start; his dynamic platoon edge against the opposing lefty, paired with a slow pitcher delivery that heavily favors his stolen base potential, allows him to provide elite category scarcity value. While **Austin Riley** (63.38) and **Max Muncy** (62.50) round out the middle, their high base projections mitigate the risks of their lower lineup positioning. We ultimately sidelined **Isaac Paredes** (53.24) in favor of these higher-ceiling plays; while he remained the closest bench candidate, the combination of a 32% rain risk and a negative platoon split against Thornton capped his utility, pushing him just below the threshold required to displace the more favorable matchups in our active slots. \n\nRisk management remains a priority, specifically regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Max Muncy**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Isaac Paredes**. Should the official cards shuffle, we may see volatility in the scoring output for **Seiya Suzuki** (51.68), whose score is currently depressed by a tough matchup against Burns. We are comfortable keeping **Cole Young** (55.52) in the 2B slot despite the lower projection, as the 100% stolen base rate projection against the opposing battery is a calculated play for the scarcest category in our 5x5 format.",
